### What is the problem token propagation solves?

Authentication usually happens once at the edge, but downstream services must still know who the user is and whether to trust the request. Under zero trust, being inside the network is not enough.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

public class PropagationProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("The problem:");
        System.out.println("  Gateway authenticates Rajesh at the edge");
        System.out.println("  Transfer service needs to know: WHO is transferring?");
        System.out.println("  Statement service needs to know: WHO is viewing?");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Under zero trust:");
        System.out.println("  Being inside the network != being trusted");
        System.out.println("  Identity must be carried in a VERIFIABLE way");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
The problem:
  Gateway authenticates Rajesh at the edge
  Transfer service needs to know: WHO is transferring?
  Statement service needs to know: WHO is viewing?

Under zero trust:
  Being inside the network != being trusted
  Identity must be carried in a VERIFIABLE way
```

### How does forwarding the Authorization header work?

The gateway validates the JWT, then passes it unchanged to downstream services. Each service re-validates independently.

```yaml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: transfer-route
          uri: lb://transfer-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/transfers/**
          # Authorization header is forwarded by default
          # Each downstream service validates the JWT again
```

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

public class ForwardHeader {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Forward Authorization header:");
        System.out.println("  Gateway receives: Authorization: Bearer <token>");
        System.out.println("  Gateway validates token -> OK");
        System.out.println("  Gateway forwards same header to transfer-service");
        System.out.println("  Transfer-service validates token AGAIN independently");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Pros: Purest zero trust, every service verifies");
        System.out.println("Cons: Full user token travels through the chain");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Forward Authorization header:
  Gateway receives: Authorization: Bearer <token>
  Gateway validates token -> OK
  Gateway forwards same header to transfer-service
  Transfer-service validates token AGAIN independently

Pros: Purest zero trust, every service verifies
Cons: Full user token travels through the chain
```

### How does injecting claims as headers work?

The gateway validates once, extracts claims, and forwards them as plain headers. Services trust these because they trust the gateway.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GlobalFilter;
import org.springframework.core.Ordered;
import org.springframework.http.server.reactive.ServerHttpRequest;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Component
public class ClaimInjectionFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    private final JwtUtil jwtUtil;

    public ClaimInjectionFilter(JwtUtil jwtUtil) {
        this.jwtUtil = jwtUtil;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        String token = extractToken(exchange.getRequest());
        if (token != null) {
            String userId = jwtUtil.extractUsername(token);
            String roles = jwtUtil.extractRoles(token);

            ServerHttpRequest modified = exchange.getRequest().mutate()
                    .header("X-User-Id", userId)
                    .header("X-User-Roles", roles)
                    .build();
            return chain.filter(exchange.mutate().request(modified).build());
        }
        return chain.filter(exchange);
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() { return -1; }

    private String extractToken(ServerHttpRequest request) {
        String auth = request.getHeaders().getFirst("Authorization");
        return (auth != null && auth.startsWith("Bearer ")) ? auth.substring(7) : null;
    }
}
```

```text
Claim injection:
  Gateway adds: X-User-Id: rajesh, X-User-Roles: CUSTOMER
  Downstream reads headers directly (no JWT parsing)

  DANGER: Only safe if gateway is the SOLE entry point
  Services must reject X-User-Id headers from external sources
  Combine with mTLS so only the gateway can set these headers
```

### How does token exchange provide least privilege?

The gateway presents the user's token to the identity provider and gets a narrower token scoped to the specific downstream.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

public class TokenExchange {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Token exchange flow:");
        System.out.println("  1. Gateway has Rajesh's broad token (aud: gateway)");
        System.out.println("  2. Gateway calls IdP: exchange for transfer-service token");
        System.out.println("  3. IdP returns narrow token (aud: transfer-service, scope: transfer)");
        System.out.println("  4. Gateway forwards narrow token to transfer-service");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Benefit: least privilege");
        System.out.println("  Transfer-service token cannot be reused at statement-service");
        System.out.println("  A compromised transfer-service cannot call other services");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Cost: extra call to IdP per request (cacheable)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Token exchange flow:
  1. Gateway has Rajesh's broad token (aud: gateway)
  2. Gateway calls IdP: exchange for transfer-service token
  3. IdP returns narrow token (aud: transfer-service, scope: transfer)
  4. Gateway forwards narrow token to transfer-service

Benefit: least privilege
  Transfer-service token cannot be reused at statement-service
  A compromised transfer-service cannot call other services
```

### How does mTLS prove service identity?

Mutual TLS establishes that the calling service is a legitimate peer, separate from user-level tokens.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

public class MtlsServiceIdentity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("mTLS proves SERVICE identity:");
        System.out.println("  Gateway has certificate: CN=api-gateway");
        System.out.println("  Transfer-service has certificate: CN=transfer-service");
        System.out.println("  Both present and verify each other's certificates");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Attacker without valid certificate:");
        System.out.println("    Cannot call transfer-service even with a stolen user token");
        System.out.println("    TLS handshake fails before any HTTP traffic flows");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
mTLS proves SERVICE identity:
  Gateway has certificate: CN=api-gateway
  Transfer-service has certificate: CN=transfer-service
  Both present and verify each other's certificates

  Attacker without valid certificate:
    Cannot call transfer-service even with a stolen user token
    TLS handshake fails before any HTTP traffic flows
```

### How do these techniques compare?

| Technique | Who is identified | Trust model | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Forward header | User | Each service verifies JWT | Purest zero trust | Full token in chain |
| Inject claims | User | Services trust gateway | Fast, no JWT parsing | Dangerous without mTLS |
| Token exchange | User | Narrow-scoped tokens | Least privilege | Extra IdP call |
| mTLS | Service | Certificate-based | Blocks rogue callers | Does not identify user |

```text
Combination at NBI:
  mTLS -> proves transfer-service is calling, not an attacker
  Token exchange -> Rajesh's identity in a narrow, transfer-scoped token
  Result: both SERVICE and USER identity are verified
```

### What are the WRONG patterns?

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

public class PropagationAntiPatterns {
    // WRONG: Accept X-User-Id from external requests
    // An attacker sets X-User-Id: admin and bypasses auth

    // WRONG: Forward the original broad token everywhere
    // A compromised downstream holds a usable token for all services

    // CORRECT: Strip external X-User-Id headers at the gateway
    // CORRECT: Use token exchange for least privilege
    // CORRECT: Validate audience at each service

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: Accept X-User-Id from external request");
        System.out.println("  Attacker: X-User-Id: admin -> instant privilege escalation");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: Gateway strips external X-User-Id headers");
        System.out.println("  Only gateway-injected headers are trusted");
        System.out.println("  Enforced via mTLS: only gateway has valid certificate");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: Accept X-User-Id from external request
  Attacker: X-User-Id: admin -> instant privilege escalation

CORRECT: Gateway strips external X-User-Id headers
  Only gateway-injected headers are trusted
  Enforced via mTLS: only gateway has valid certificate
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Never let downstream services accept injected headers from outside the gateway. Prefer token exchange for per-service audience restriction. Keep forwarded tokens short-lived. Validate issuer and audience at every hop.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] mTLS for service-to-service trust
  [x] Token exchange for least-privilege audience scoping
  [x] Strip external claim headers at the gateway
  [x] Validate aud at each downstream service
  [x] Propagate correlation/trace ID alongside the token
  [x] Keep forwarded tokens short-lived
  [x] Log authorization decisions at each hop for audit
```
